Z||000000||Harry||01-03-2000||01:28 PM||||Well after much reading from all our helpful members, asking more questions than a 4 year old, and some careful techno try outs (your idea's) I have come to the one and only conclusion possible.<BR>Either a: the last @#$%^ who changed the clutch in my GT did not replace the throw out bearing. Or b: all the traffic I deal with every day has just plain wiped it out.<BR>Yes the clutch is good,the Master good,the slave new. Yet the problem get's worse by the week.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Harry||01-03-2000||01:35 PM||||My T.O. bearing has started to make some noise and seems like every week the clutch is disengaging less and less. After much contemplation over (clutchs revisited) and some serious talk with Pontiac techs we have deduced that the bearing is bad.<BR>I just wanted to thank you all for the very informative speculations,trials and error's listed here.<BR>I hope each one of you has a very Happy year 2000 <p>[This message has been edited by Harry (edited 01-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||01-04-2000||03:34 PM||||Harry-  I ran into  a similar problem.  Took everything apart, replaced everything master, slave, clutch , throwout and it still didn't shift.  It turned out that the one item I didn't replace, the clutch arm (the lever that the slave piston pushes when you push in the clutch) was bad.  That piece was cracked and I couldn't see the crack at first.  I had the old original pressed type. Well I replaced it with the cast iron version and voila that was it.  It made me sick that I went through all that hassle but I did learn a lot about my car and how to take it apart and put it back together again, without too many extra nuts and bolts left over. Check that item before you go any further.  Pontiac has it and it takes 15-30 minutes to replace.  ||||reg||12
